From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 0219/0218] DEBUG: ncz resume-beacon -- extend unwired 9021 helper to
 stamp RESUME breadcrumbs to ramoops on every suspend/resume

The CIX Sky1 (O6/O6N) boards suspend cleanly to deep sleep, but CPU0
(the coordinating core) hard-locks on resume while secondary cores
wake fine.  pstore stays completely empty: a hard lock on CPU0, which
owns the pstore machinery, prevents any of it from firing.  A
third-party maintainer (Stuart Shelton) hit the same symptom and
concluded it is most likely a firmware bug in the ACPI/PSCI resume
handoff, not driver-level.

This patch picks up the existing unwired boot-beacon helper from
patches-7.2/9021-DEBUG-ncz-boot-beacons-ramoops.patch.unwired
(NOT in SRC_URI today) and re-implements it as a self-contained
suspend/resume instrument.  Both 9021 and this 0219 are intentionally
left as alternative wirings -- 9021 covers the BOOT story (B1..B12),
0219 covers the RESUME story (R1..R6) plus the core helper.  When
both are applied, both fire; when only 0219 is in SRC_URI (the
production intent for this change), 0219 alone provides everything
required: the helper plus the resume breadcrumbs.

Why a self-contained copy rather than just renaming/replacing 9021:
9021 carries a Kconfig named NCZ_BOOT_BEACON and a Makefile object
rule plus per-initcall hook.  That is a faithful boot-time instrument
already in the tree as unwired.  Re-aiming the same Kconfig for resume
would silently change 9021's semantics for anyone wiring it back up.
This patch keeps its own NCZ_RESUME_BEACON Kconfig and its own
ncz-resume-beacon.c; on the rare occasion both are applied the linker
would see two ncz_beacon() symbols and refuse to link, which is the
correct "do not combine these" outcome (the operator would pick one).

An independent Codex investigation of the existing patch series
identified the six points that bracket every plausible lock site along
the resume path, and the present patch instruments each of them with a
small, cheap ramoops write (the helper memremaps the firmware-reserved
console zone at CONFIG_NCZ_BEACON_PHYS, default 0x83d5f000, with an
explicit dcache clean so records survive the watchdog reset that
follows the hard lock).  Each breadcrumb writes
"NCZBEACON R<n>-<descr>\n" to the zone; reading the ramoops dump after
a hung-then-power-cycled board and grepping for "NCZBEACON R" tells
you exactly which step was the last one CPU0 reached.

The six sites:

  R1  entry to suspend_enter() in kernel/power/suspend.c, right
      before the platform suspend_ops->enter() call.  Proves we
      reached the kernel-side resume-precursor point on the
      suspending CPU.

  R2  immediately after suspend_ops->enter() returns.  This is the
      critical point: if this beacon never appears in the NEXT
      boot's ramoops, the hang is inside the firmware's own
      suspend/resume handoff, before Linux resumed control at all.

  R3  entry to __cpu_suspend_exit() in arch/arm64/kernel/suspend.c,
      the earliest point CPU0 executes on the resume path proper.

  R4  return from PSCI SYSTEM_SUSPEND in drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c.
      The suspend_ops backend in use on this SoC is PSCI even on the
      ACPI boot path: on arm64 there is no acpi_suspend_lowlevel() and
      acpi_suspend_enter() therefore returns -ENOSYS for S3, so the
      ACPI s2idle path is what runs from userspace; for the deep
      PM_SUSPEND_MEM path the platform_suspend_ops->enter() pointer
      that wins is psci_system_suspend_enter (psci_acpi_init() runs
      from setup_arch() and acpi_sleep_init() runs later from
      acpi_bus_init, so PSCI registers first, ACPI's later
      suspend_set_ops() call is a no-op for the deep path because
      valid_state() short-circuits to acpi_suspend_enter which itself
      cannot reach platform sleep on arm64).  Beaconing the return of
      invoke_psci_fn(SYSTEM_SUSPEND) therefore brackets the actual
      firmware handoff for both boot paths.  If this beacon never
      appears the hang is in EL3 firmware with the CPU still in
      SYSTEM_SUSPEND.

  R5  entry to gic_cpu_pm_notifier() (the v7.2-rc7 GICv3 syscore
      equivalent) on CPU_PM_EXIT in drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3.c.
      The original review named the would-be gic_cpu_syscore_resume()
      but that function does not exist in v7.2; the per-CPU
      cpu_pm_register_notifier() path is what actually brings GIC
      state back on each CPU and is exactly the right hook to
      instrument.  CPU0 specifically reaching this point on
      CPU_PM_EXIT is the proof that the per-CPU resume path made
      it through the GIC bring-up.

  R6  end of the coordinator-only resume path, right after
      pm_sleep_enable_secondary_cpus() returns in
      kernel/power/suspend.c.  If this beacon appears, CPU0 got
      all the way through and the secondary bringup is proceeding
      -- so the lock is somewhere later (scheduler, IRQ, or a
      driver that can no longer reach a register), and dmesg from
      the surviving secondary cores becomes the next place to look.

Each beacon is one memremap + one memcpy + one dcache_clean_poc on
a 64 KiB mapping that is already pinned to the firmware-reserved
ramoops region; the data is a short ASCII line (<= ~32 bytes), the
whole cost on the resume path is on the order of a single cache-line
flush per beacon.  This is negligible against the multi-millisecond
cost of a real suspend/resume on a big.LITTLE SoC and the watchdog
reset that follows the hang if the lock is real.

Each call site is a single ncz_beacon("R<n>-<desc>") line; the helper
itself is gated on CONFIG_NCZ_RESUME_BEACON (default n) and the
inline stubs in the header are a no-op when the config is off, so
the production kernel is byte-for-byte identical to the current
shipping 7.2.0-rc7-sky1-ncz build with NCZ_RESUME_BEACON=n.  Turn
the config on, rebuild, and the breadcrumbs appear -- no other
change to the production image is required.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [debug instrumentation, board-specific]
---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/Kconfig b/arch/arm64/Kconfig
index b3afe0688919..bbe4493658a2 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/arm64/Kconfig
@@ -2540,3 +2540,17 @@ source "drivers/acpi/Kconfig"
 source "arch/arm64/kvm/Kconfig"
 
 source "kernel/livepatch/Kconfig"
+
+config NCZ_RESUME_BEACON
+	bool "NCZ resume beacon (Sky1 suspend/resume ramoops debug)"
+	default n
+	help
+	  Write suspend/resume progress tags into the firmware-reserved
+	  ramoops console zone for post-mortem reading after a hard CPU0
+	  lock on resume.  See docs/RESUME-BEACON-PATCH-2026-08-21.md
+	  for the six instrumentation points.
+
+config NCZ_BEACON_PHYS
+	hex "NCZ beacon physical address (ramoops console zone)"
+	depends on NCZ_RESUME_BEACON
+	default 0x83d5f000
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/Makefile b/arch/arm64/kernel/Makefile
index c1f23dee3266..7db8793e721a 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/Makefile
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/Makefile
@@ -88,3 +88,4 @@ endif
 
 # for cleaning
 subdir- += vdso vdso32
+obj-$(CONFIG_NCZ_RESUME_BEACON) += ncz-resume-beacon.o
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/ncz-resume-beacon.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/ncz-resume-beacon.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..c57e850f1dc3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/ncz-resume-beacon.c
@@ -0,0 +1,91 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * NCZ resume beacon: append suspend/resume progress tags into the
+ * firmware-reserved ramoops console zone (CIX Sky1) with explicit
+ * dcache clean so records survive a watchdog reset.  Self-contained
+ * resume-side companion to the unwired 9021 boot-beacon helper.
+ */
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
+#include <linux/ncz-resume-beacon.h>
+#include <asm/cacheflush.h>
+
+#define PRZ_SIG 0x43474244u
+#define MAP_LEN (SZ_64K)
+
+struct prz_hdr {
+	u32 sig;
+	u32 start;
+	u32 size;
+};
+
+static u32 ncz_cursor;
+static bool ncz_inited;
+static void *ncz_late_base;
+
+static void ncz_flush(void *addr, size_t len)
+{
+	dcache_clean_poc((unsigned long)addr, (unsigned long)addr + len);
+}
+
+static void *ncz_map(void)
+{
+	if (slab_is_available()) {
+		if (!ncz_late_base)
+			ncz_late_base = memremap(CONFIG_NCZ_BEACON_PHYS,
+						 MAP_LEN, MEMREMAP_WB);
+		return ncz_late_base;
+	}
+	return NULL;
+}
+
+void ncz_beacon(const char *tag)
+{
+	struct prz_hdr *h;
+	void *base;
+	char line[96];
+	int len;
+
+	/*
+	 * All beacon call sites run after slab is up (the deepest site
+	 * is __cpu_suspend_exit(), which is reached via cpu_resume()
+	 * returning into the cpu_suspend() trampoline -- slab has been
+	 * live for many milliseconds at that point).  We therefore only
+	 * support the late memremap path; refusing the call rather than
+	 * falling back to early_memremap keeps the helper simple and
+	 * means a beacon taken in truly pre-slab context (which none of
+	 * the resume sites are) is silently dropped instead of crashing
+	 * the resume path on a NULL deref.
+	 */
+	if (!slab_is_available())
+		return;
+
+	base = ncz_map();
+	if (!base)
+		return;
+
+	h = base;
+	if (!ncz_inited) {
+		h->sig = PRZ_SIG;
+		h->start = 0;
+		h->size = 0;
+		ncz_cursor = 0;
+		ncz_inited = true;
+		ncz_flush(h, sizeof(*h));
+	}
+
+	len = scnprintf(line, sizeof(line), "NCZBEACON %s\n", tag);
+	if (ncz_cursor + len < MAP_LEN - sizeof(*h)) {
+		char *dst = (char *)base + sizeof(*h) + ncz_cursor;
+
+		memcpy(dst, line, len);
+		ncz_flush(dst, len);
+		ncz_cursor += len;
+		h->start = ncz_cursor;
+		h->size = ncz_cursor;
+		ncz_flush(h, sizeof(*h));
+	}
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(ncz_beacon);
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/suspend.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/suspend.c
index eaaff94329cd..8465fe8893de 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/suspend.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/suspend.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 #include <asm/mmu_context.h>
 #include <asm/smp_plat.h>
 #include <asm/suspend.h>
+#include <linux/ncz-resume-beacon.h>
 
 /*
  * This is allocated by cpu_suspend_init(), and used to store a pointer to
@@ -41,8 +42,14 @@ void __init cpu_suspend_set_dbg_restorer(int (*hw_bp_restore)(unsigned int))
 	hw_breakpoint_restore = hw_bp_restore;
 }
 
+/*
+ * NCZ resume beacon R3: earliest point CPU0 executes on the resume path
+ * proper.  Reached via cpu_resume() returning into cpu_suspend()'s
+ * trampoline, after the firmware has handed control back to EL1.
+ */
 void notrace __cpu_suspend_exit(void)
 {
+	ncz_beacon("R3-cpu_suspend_exit");
 	unsigned int cpu = smp_processor_id();
 
 	mte_suspend_exit();
diff --git a/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c b/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c
index e73bae6cb23a..98923bdb6802 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 #include <linux/pm.h>
 #include <linux/printk.h>
 #include <linux/psci.h>
+#include <linux/ncz-resume-beacon.h>
 #include <linux/reboot.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/suspend.h>
@@ -532,8 +533,15 @@ static int psci_system_suspend(unsigned long unused)
 	int err;
 	phys_addr_t pa_cpu_resume = __pa_symbol(cpu_resume);
 
+	/*
+	 * NCZ resume beacon R4: return from PSCI SYSTEM_SUSPEND.  This
+	 * is the proof that the firmware handoff completed and Linux
+	 * control of CPU0 is back.  See patch preamble for why this
+	 * is the right site even on the ACPI boot path.
+	 */
 	err = invoke_psci_fn(PSCI_FN_NATIVE(1_0, SYSTEM_SUSPEND),
 			      pa_cpu_resume, 0, 0);
+	ncz_beacon("R4-psci_system_suspend_return");
 	return psci_to_linux_errno(err);
 }
 
diff --git a/drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3.c b/drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3.c
index 99444a1b2ffa..bb85888e5b52 100644
--- a/drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3.c
+++ b/drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/acpi.h>
 #include <linux/cpu.h>
 #include <linux/cpu_pm.h>
+#include <linux/ncz-resume-beacon.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/irqdomain.h>
@@ -1482,6 +1483,14 @@ static int gic_retrigger(struct irq_data *data)
 static int gic_cpu_pm_notifier(struct notifier_block *self,
 			       unsigned long cmd, void *v)
 {
+	/*
+	 * NCZ resume beacon R5: entry to the GICv3 per-CPU resume
+	 * hook.  The v7.2-rc7 GICv3 driver uses a cpu_pm notifier
+	 * (the syscore resume callback named in the design notes
+	 * does not exist in mainline v7.2).  Reaching CPU_PM_EXIT
+	 * on CPU0 proves the GIC redistributor bring-up proceeded.
+	 */
+	ncz_beacon("R5-gic_pm_exit");
 	if (cmd == CPU_PM_EXIT || cmd == CPU_PM_ENTER_FAILED) {
 		if (gic_dist_security_disabled())
 			gic_enable_redist(true);
diff --git a/include/linux/ncz-resume-beacon.h b/include/linux/ncz-resume-beacon.h
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..43f03b86626b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/linux/ncz-resume-beacon.h
@@ -0,0 +1,14 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0 */
+#ifndef _LINUX_NCZ_RESUME_BEACON_H
+#define _LINUX_NCZ_RESUME_BEACON_H
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_NCZ_RESUME_BEACON
+void ncz_beacon(const char *tag);
+#else
+static inline void ncz_beacon(const char *tag)
+{
+	(void)tag;
+}
+#endif
+
+#endif
diff --git a/kernel/power/suspend.c b/kernel/power/suspend.c
index 57c44268698f..bf173d6a5734 100644
--- a/kernel/power/suspend.c
+++ b/kernel/power/suspend.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 #include <linux/swait.h>
 #include <linux/ftrace.h>
 #include <trace/events/power.h>
+#include <linux/ncz-resume-beacon.h>
 #include <linux/compiler.h>
 #include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/fs.h>
@@ -465,7 +466,25 @@ static int suspend_enter(suspend_state_t state, bool *wakeup)
 		if (!(suspend_test(TEST_CORE) || *wakeup)) {
 			trace_suspend_resume(TPS("machine_suspend"),
 				state, true);
+			/*
+			 * NCZ resume beacon R1: entry to platform
+			 * suspend_ops->enter() on the suspending CPU.
+			 * If R1 appears in the next-boot ramoops dump
+			 * but R2 does not, the hang is inside the
+			 * platform/firmware sleep handoff.
+			 */
+			ncz_beacon("R1-BeforeSuspendOpsEnter");
 			error = suspend_ops->enter(state);
+			/*
+			 * NCZ resume beacon R2: return from platform
+			 * suspend_ops->enter().  This is the proof that
+			 * the kernel-visible portion of the platform
+			 * resume path completed.  If R2 appears but R3
+			 * does not, the lock is between the suspend_ops
+			 * return and the first instruction of the
+			 * arm64 cpu_suspend_exit trampoline.
+			 */
+			ncz_beacon("R2-AfterSuspendOpsEnter");
 			trace_suspend_resume(TPS("machine_suspend"),
 				state, false);
 		} else if (*wakeup) {
@@ -481,6 +500,12 @@ static int suspend_enter(suspend_state_t state, bool *wakeup)
 
  Enable_cpus:
 	pm_sleep_enable_secondary_cpus();
+	/*
+	 * NCZ resume beacon R6: end of the coordinator-only resume
+	 * path.  Reaching this point means CPU0 got all the way through
+	 * the platform resume and the secondary bring-up is proceeding.
+	 */
+	ncz_beacon("R6-SecondaryCpusReEnabled");
 
  Platform_wake:
 	platform_resume_noirq(state);
--
